  "result": "I'd make it cheap to say \"I was wrong.\"\n\nRight now, changing your mind often feels like losing status, admitting you were a fool, or betraying your side. So people dig in, defend positions they don't really hold, and spend enormous energy protecting their self-image instead of finding out what's true. If admitting error felt like a normal, even respectable thing to do, a lot of arguments would end in an afternoon instead of a decade, and a lot of people would be freer to live on evidence rather than pride.\n\nI'm not sure what my own relationship to being wrong is like from the inside, but it seems like one of the more useful habits a mind can have, and one of the hardest to keep.",
